<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boten–Vientiane railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Laos–China Railway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Boten–Vientiane railway, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> Railway, is a railway of about 420 kilometres from the Chinese border at Boten to Vientiane, the capital of Laos. Built by Chinese companies under the Belt and Road Initiative, with many tunnels and bridges through the mountains, it opened in December 2021 and connects with the line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a>. It is the largest infrastructure project in the country's history and added to its debts to China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boten-vientiane-railway.jpg" alt="Boten–Vientiane railway" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Embassy of Timor-Leste in Vientiane · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2021-12-03_China-Laos-Eisenbahn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> is the only landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and under French rule only the 7-kilometre Don Det–Don Khon railway was built there. Talks on a railway linking Laos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> began in 2001, and the Lao politician Somsavat Lengsavad was reportedly the driving force on the Laotian side. Plans for a standard-gauge line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xishuangbanna">Xishuangbanna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> were announced in October 2010, but the project stalled after the 2011 corruption scandal involving China's minister of railways, Liu Zhijun. In 2015 the two countries agreed to finance and operate it jointly under a build-operate-transfer arrangement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Construction began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> on 25 December 2016, and unexploded bombs dropped during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> were removed along the route. Track-laying was completed on 12 October 2021, and the line opened on 3 December 2021, a day after the 46th anniversary of the Lao PDR. About 47% of it runs through 75 tunnels and 15% over viaducts on 167 bridges. It is a single electrified track with passing loops, built to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s Class I trunk standards for passenger trains at 160 km/h and freight at 120 km/h. Its northern end joins the Yuxi–Mohan railway at Mohan, and the largest station, in Vientiane, has four platforms.</p><p>The cost was estimated at US$5.965 billion. Sixty per cent was financed by loans from the Export–Import Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the remaining 40% by a joint venture of the two countries in which China holds a 70% stake. Of the Laotian share, the government paid $250 million from its national budget and borrowed a further $480 million from the same bank, and Western publications later claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> could default on its debts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The line was expected to bring tourists, with passengers making up most of its traffic, and to cut the cost of Thai fruit exports to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A branch to the Thanaleng Dry Port and Vientiane South, completed in July 2022, allowed cargo to pass between the Thai metre-gauge and the Chinese standard-gauge lines, and a bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> is planned to link the line with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>–Nong Khai high-speed railway. Radio Free Asia reported in October 2021 that some villagers displaced by the construction had not been compensated; by August 2023 it reported that about 85% of affected families had had their claims resolved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boten–Vientiane railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Growth from hydropower dams and mining, and the Chinese-built <b>Laos-China railway</b> opened in 2021, have deepened ties with China, alongside heavy public debt.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-peoples-democratic-republic">Lao People's Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
